 "name": "merge_faces_geometry_parity",
 "face_ring_start": "ignored",
 "face_ring_start_note": "Every case here merges faces, and the merged n-gon's ring start is the reference behaviour this family does not reproduce: measured 2026-08-18, the merged face's ring is identical and identically wound on both sides in all eleven cases and begins at a different corner in all eleven. The fixture's question is which faces the merge leaves and which corners they hold; where the walk that builds that ring begins is a separate, unread law -- doc/tasks/backlog/1311-ring-start-laws-to-capture.md.",
 "description": "Merging a selected face set into one polygon: what the boundary walk does with a vertex that becomes strictly interior, with a non-planar pair, and with an L / U shaped region. GEOMETRY CHANNEL ONLY (the capture recorded a differing post-operation selection).",
 "source": "frozen reference capture (task 1121, nine-tool sweep)",
 "provenance": {
  "schema": 1,
  "source": "live-capture",
  "reference": "ref-editor@11.2v3",
  "method": "command",
  "captured_utc": "2026-08-18",
  "harness": "tools/local/capture (tasks 1121/1123)",
  "task": "1160",
  "notes": "The pole cells are the separation: merging a whole fan makes the hub interior, and whether an interior vertex is kept as a collinear corner or deleted is exactly the question a closed solid cannot ask. CHANNELS: the sweep's comparison was never vertex-only -- it compared the vertex multiset, the edge multiset, faces BOTH as ordered rings (winding shows) and as unordered position sets, the material partition, the per-face 'did this face's own tag change', and the post-command selection. Re-checked independently over all 215 frozen cases: ZERO agree on vertices while differing on ordered face rings, so nothing frozen here is a vertex-channel-only agreement. PRECISION: the sweep compared at 1e-4 and this fixture's tolerance matches it. The actual reproduction is far tighter -- 208 of the 215 cases are bit-identical at the dumps' 6 decimals and the remaining 7 agree to 3e-6 or better, that being the worst deviation anywhere in the set. The tolerance is the MEASUREMENT's bound, not the data's."
 },
